1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,626
This is Lunar Site Planner.

2
00:00:01,626 --> 00:00:07,286
It helps you choose where and when to land near the Moon's south pole, by checking two things:

3
00:00:07,286 --> 00:00:10,176
sunlight for power, and Earth in view for radio.

4
00:00:10,876 --> 00:00:14,007
Professional mode puts everything on one screen.

5
00:00:14,007 --> 00:00:16,160
On the left, pick a landing site:

6
00:00:16,160 --> 00:00:21,052
Apollo, commercial landers, or spots we found in NASA's laser terrain maps.

7
00:00:21,752 --> 00:00:23,763
The 3D Moon uses NASA imagery.

8
00:00:23,763 --> 00:00:28,052
Gold points to the Sun from your site, and blue points to Earth.

9
00:00:28,052 --> 00:00:32,141
Spin it with the middle mouse button, just like CAD software.

10
00:00:32,841 --> 00:00:35,401
This status pill gives the verdict right now:

11
00:00:35,401 --> 00:00:39,838
how much power the solar panels make, and how high Earth sits above the hills.

12
00:00:40,538 --> 00:00:47,602
We also search fifty-nine candidate sites for the best spot overall, and the best place a mission has already landed.

13
00:00:47,602 --> 00:00:50,138
The dotted lines show their link to Earth.

14
00:00:50,838 --> 00:00:52,930
Press play to travel through time.

15
00:00:52,930 --> 00:00:57,729
At the south pole, the Sun skims the horizon and circles the sky once a month.

16
00:00:58,429 --> 00:01:00,892
The timeline shows the next thirty days:

17
00:01:00,892 --> 00:01:05,448
sunlight, power, Earth, the Deep Space Network, and the direct radio link.

18
00:01:06,148 --> 00:01:11,865
On the right, the assessment counts down to sunset and Earthset, and draws the real terrain skyline.

19
00:01:12,565 --> 00:01:13,421
Launches slip.

20
00:01:13,421 --> 00:01:18,432
The compare tab tests the same site one, two and three months later, side by side.

21
00:01:19,132 --> 00:01:20,030
New to all this?

22
00:01:20,030 --> 00:01:23,676
Beginner mode walks you through it one step at a time, with Lumi.
